WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.795
- E-sports is going to
become as big as football,

00:00:02.795 --> 00:00:04.063
or soccer or baseball,

00:00:04.063 --> 00:00:06.367
and I don't know, that's
going to be really up

00:00:06.367 --> 00:00:07.590
to the players to decide.

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:08.931
- [Marc] Players do things
with their champions

00:00:08.931 --> 00:00:11.034
we'd never even imagine,
the designers would imagine,

00:00:11.034 --> 00:00:13.500
and we sort of evolved that understanding

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.280
together with the community.

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:17.414
- [Commentator] Missing, oh the flash!

00:00:17.414 --> 00:00:18.764
They got him.

00:00:18.764 --> 00:00:20.344
- Whether you think you can do it,

00:00:20.344 --> 00:00:23.072
or you think you can not, you are right.

00:00:23.072 --> 00:00:24.780
- What's up, believe nation?

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.205
It's Evan, my one word is "believe",

00:00:27.205 --> 00:00:28.874
and I believe that you have the ability

00:00:28.874 --> 00:00:31.205
to do something special
that can change the planet,

00:00:31.205 --> 00:00:32.580
so to help you on your journey,

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:34.996
today we're going to learn
from the CEO of Riot Games,

00:00:34.996 --> 00:00:39.163
Brandon Beck and my takes on
his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:46.447
(inspiring classical music)

00:00:52.556 --> 00:00:54.657
- You have to have an
almost idealistic belief

00:00:54.657 --> 00:00:56.944
in what you're doing because if you

00:00:56.944 --> 00:00:59.731
take a linear, logical perspective

00:00:59.731 --> 00:01:02.999
on what you're trying to
accomplish as a start-up,

00:01:02.999 --> 00:01:05.749
if that makes sense, it's almost,

00:01:06.958 --> 00:01:09.588
the start is almost not ambitious enough

00:01:09.588 --> 00:01:11.394
and if it doesn't make sense then it

00:01:11.394 --> 00:01:13.165
can be like staring into the abyss of

00:01:13.165 --> 00:01:14.899
how the hell is this ever going to work?

00:01:14.899 --> 00:01:17.649
And there just sort of needs to be,

00:01:20.034 --> 00:01:22.603
that old saying where there's a will,

00:01:22.603 --> 00:01:24.604
there's a way, there just needs to be that

00:01:24.604 --> 00:01:26.971
belief almost idealistically, almost

00:01:26.971 --> 00:01:28.944
not based in rational thinking,

00:01:28.944 --> 00:01:33.085
that we can sort of
pull this off, somehow.

00:01:33.085 --> 00:01:36.092
And at least for us that was,

00:01:36.092 --> 00:01:38.583
that was key, believing in sort of a

00:01:38.583 --> 00:01:40.550
non-linear path to success.

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:41.989
It's hard for us to sort of resonate with

00:01:41.989 --> 00:01:44.476
how well the game has been doing,

00:01:44.476 --> 00:01:47.278
and it's like the ultimate compliment.

00:01:47.278 --> 00:01:48.875
It is the best feeling in the world

00:01:48.875 --> 00:01:51.552
to see players enthusiastic
about the product.

00:01:51.552 --> 00:01:52.792
And like, well you guys are here for the

00:01:52.792 --> 00:01:54.294
world finals coming up tomorrow,

00:01:54.294 --> 00:01:58.324
the, I mean, that is like
a dream come true for us.

00:01:58.324 --> 00:02:02.834
We've always wanted E-sports
to be at that scale,

00:02:02.834 --> 00:02:04.300
but we never even believed that that

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:05.663
would be possible.

00:02:05.663 --> 00:02:09.350
I remember one day like
we were in a team meeting,

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:11.327
we just got the results
from the survey back

00:02:11.327 --> 00:02:13.673
and we'd ask players if
they enjoy watching the game

00:02:13.673 --> 00:02:15.167
in addition to playing it.

00:02:15.167 --> 00:02:17.933
And the number was, something like 70%

00:02:17.933 --> 00:02:20.741
of players enjoyed watching
it, and we're like,

00:02:20.741 --> 00:02:21.652
no way, that has to be wrong.

00:02:21.652 --> 00:02:23.715
We enjoy watching it,
but that's what we do,

00:02:23.715 --> 00:02:25.077
we make games, like there's no 70%,

00:02:25.077 --> 00:02:28.696
and in fact, that was an accurate number,

00:02:28.696 --> 00:02:30.945
and then it was validated by the sort

00:02:30.945 --> 00:02:33.186
of the viewership numbers
for our first tournament,

00:02:33.186 --> 00:02:35.897
we were like, holy crap, green light

00:02:35.897 --> 00:02:38.814
for going ape on making phenomenal,

00:02:39.667 --> 00:02:43.467
on building a phenomenal
e-sports experience.

00:02:43.467 --> 00:02:46.298
And that's been so much
fun to just run with.

00:02:46.298 --> 00:02:47.832
We want the sport to be big and awesome,

00:02:47.832 --> 00:02:49.734
but I want to make one distinction clear,

00:02:49.734 --> 00:02:52.497
we're not trying to make
this a mainstream sport,

00:02:52.497 --> 00:02:53.689
a lot of people ask us,

00:02:53.689 --> 00:02:57.145
hey guys, are e-sports
going to become as big

00:02:57.145 --> 00:03:00.516
as football or soccer or baseball?

00:03:00.516 --> 00:03:01.882
And I don't know, that's
going to be really

00:03:01.882 --> 00:03:03.234
up to the players to decide.

00:03:03.234 --> 00:03:05.382
But that's not a specific goal.

00:03:05.382 --> 00:03:07.034
But the goal is to
deliver a great experience

00:03:07.034 --> 00:03:08.601
for the already large community

00:03:08.601 --> 00:03:10.081
that really cares about it,

00:03:10.081 --> 00:03:11.437
so that's the focus, the goal isn't to

00:03:11.437 --> 00:03:15.268
make e-sports as mass
market or big as possible.

00:03:15.268 --> 00:03:16.528
I think that's kind of just forcing,

00:03:16.528 --> 00:03:19.131
trying to force it, and it
has to happen organically.

00:03:19.131 --> 00:03:23.298
Well this is the 2004 USA
men's national basketball team.

00:03:26.123 --> 00:03:29.626
And these guys were stacked, right?

00:03:29.626 --> 00:03:32.851
US was talent stacked on
talent, stacked on talent,

00:03:32.851 --> 00:03:35.512
and you got LeBron, you got Dwayne Wade,

00:03:35.512 --> 00:03:37.804
you got Carmelo Anthony.

00:03:37.804 --> 00:03:40.441
You got Duncan, Iverson,
and this would have been

00:03:40.441 --> 00:03:44.970
the highest paid team in the
history of pro-sports, right?

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:49.844
But does anyone remember
what happened in Athens?

00:03:49.844 --> 00:03:53.234
Right, they got dumped on by Puerto Rico,

00:03:53.234 --> 00:03:55.217
and then they lost subsequent games to

00:03:55.217 --> 00:03:57.702
Lithuania, and Argentina.

00:03:57.702 --> 00:04:00.607
They weren't supposed
to lose a single game,

00:04:00.607 --> 00:04:02.441
they were supposed to run the entire table

00:04:02.441 --> 00:04:04.079
to the Gold Medal.

00:04:04.079 --> 00:04:06.412
They were massive favorites.

00:04:07.442 --> 00:04:12.126
So while talent can win
you games (chuckles),

00:04:12.126 --> 00:04:15.376
it is teamwork that wins championships.

00:04:16.278 --> 00:04:20.190
Back in high school, I
was diagnosed with ADHD.

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:23.455
Sot he classroom was a
pretty painful place.

00:04:23.455 --> 00:04:25.463
It was hard work to spend more than half

00:04:25.463 --> 00:04:27.861
a minute's attention in any lecture

00:04:27.861 --> 00:04:31.320
regardless of how much I
enjoyed the subject matter.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:33.190
And by the way, I mention this

00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:35.421
not because I'm seeking sympathy,

00:04:35.421 --> 00:04:37.525
but I want to emphasize that we all

00:04:37.525 --> 00:04:39.871
have different handicaps, and it's how

00:04:39.871 --> 00:04:43.325
we choose to handle them
that's what's important.

00:04:43.325 --> 00:04:45.600
We can be paralyzed by self-pity,

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:48.158
we can lower our expectations,

00:04:48.158 --> 00:04:50.823
we can give up entirely.

00:04:50.823 --> 00:04:53.122
But those of us who
choose to power through

00:04:53.122 --> 00:04:55.979
and simply opt for redoubling our efforts,

00:04:55.979 --> 00:04:58.962
improve our chances for
success dramatically.

00:04:58.962 --> 00:05:01.964
And I think to really
make something great,

00:05:01.964 --> 00:05:04.704
you have to be passionate about it.

00:05:04.704 --> 00:05:07.594
It comes into like, even
like hiring criteria,

00:05:07.594 --> 00:05:08.797
when people joining Riot Games,

00:05:08.797 --> 00:05:10.201
there's two things we look for:

00:05:10.201 --> 00:05:12.402
one is just their aptitude, their talent,

00:05:12.402 --> 00:05:14.607
and then the other thing is their passion,

00:05:14.607 --> 00:05:16.003
and we care way more
about those two things

00:05:16.003 --> 00:05:18.265
than like their experience for example,

00:05:18.265 --> 00:05:20.578
because we think that you have to love,

00:05:20.578 --> 00:05:21.902
you have to love what you do,

00:05:21.902 --> 00:05:22.977
you have to love the game,

00:05:22.977 --> 00:05:24.470
you have to understand the game too,

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:26.436
'cause it's easy to come in having been

00:05:26.436 --> 00:05:27.687
a really experienced artist or really

00:05:27.687 --> 00:05:28.921
experienced game designer,

00:05:28.921 --> 00:05:30.677
but have no comprehension for

00:05:30.677 --> 00:05:33.276
how League of Legends is so different

00:05:33.276 --> 00:05:34.759
from a lot of conventional games,

00:05:34.759 --> 00:05:36.532
or a lot of conventional
wisdom breaks down.

00:05:36.532 --> 00:05:40.199
Weak culture can allow
people to just drift.

00:05:41.373 --> 00:05:44.943
And a strong culture can sort of act

00:05:44.943 --> 00:05:47.881
as this membrane that keeps large, diverse

00:05:47.881 --> 00:05:50.838
groups of people working in alignment.

00:05:50.838 --> 00:05:53.616
In every company, every
company has a culture.

00:05:53.616 --> 00:05:55.772
Whether they acknowledge
and are deliberate

00:05:55.772 --> 00:05:57.832
about it or not.

00:05:57.832 --> 00:06:00.497
Culture is sort of this living, breathing,

00:06:00.497 --> 00:06:02.785
evolving idea and it takes consistent

00:06:02.785 --> 00:06:05.491
effort over time to synthesize,

00:06:05.491 --> 00:06:08.824
to codify, and then ultimately to teach.

00:06:10.913 --> 00:06:13.162
And I love this analogy,

00:06:13.162 --> 00:06:16.174
and I'm just going to
take a survey real quick,

00:06:16.174 --> 00:06:18.034
but who in this audience is like me,

00:06:18.034 --> 00:06:20.589
and you, raise your
hand if you really like

00:06:20.589 --> 00:06:21.839
black licorice.

00:06:22.776 --> 00:06:24.417
Okay, handful of you guys.

00:06:24.417 --> 00:06:26.456
A lot of people right there.

00:06:26.456 --> 00:06:28.290
Now, who in this audience looks

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:30.898
at this image like, that
is freaking disgusting,

00:06:30.898 --> 00:06:32.313
I hate black licorice?

00:06:32.313 --> 00:06:33.466
Tons of you guys.

00:06:33.466 --> 00:06:35.893
(chuckles) Now who in this audience is

00:06:35.893 --> 00:06:38.781
really ambivalent, ah
black licorice, whatever,

00:06:38.781 --> 00:06:41.005
I have it sometimes, okay.

00:06:41.005 --> 00:06:42.595
Very few of you, right?

00:06:42.595 --> 00:06:44.928
Now, who in this audience is

00:06:46.238 --> 00:06:49.155
ambivalent about vanilla ice cream?

00:06:50.317 --> 00:06:51.492
You don't hate it, it's not the

00:06:51.492 --> 00:06:54.666
first thing you order at
Baskin N' Robbins either.

00:06:54.666 --> 00:06:56.420
Okay, almost everyone, right?

00:06:56.420 --> 00:06:59.752
And that's kind of the difference here,

00:06:59.752 --> 00:07:04.245
as a culture, you don't
want to be plain vanilla.

00:07:04.245 --> 00:07:07.142
You don't want to be a catch-all flavor

00:07:07.142 --> 00:07:10.309
that is something for everyone, right?

00:07:11.671 --> 00:07:14.887
That's dangerous, it just
waters down your culture.

00:07:14.887 --> 00:07:17.064
You want to be black licorice.

00:07:17.064 --> 00:07:19.557
You want to be cilantro.

00:07:19.557 --> 00:07:22.308
You want to be Uni, you
want to be Brussel sprouts,

00:07:22.308 --> 00:07:23.814
you want to stand for something, right?

00:07:23.814 --> 00:07:28.461
And then you'll be a
magnet for cultural fits.

00:07:28.461 --> 00:07:31.589
And others will self-select out.

00:07:31.589 --> 00:07:33.773
And Riot was an ambitious endeavor for us,

00:07:33.773 --> 00:07:36.780
to say the least, but it
was a pursuit of passion.

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:38.151
We've always loved video games,

00:07:38.151 --> 00:07:40.220
and we saw an opportunity
to do things differently,

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:41.914
and improve the traditional model.

00:07:41.914 --> 00:07:45.113
High quality, high-production
value video games

00:07:45.113 --> 00:07:48.014
had always been in this
packaged goods business,

00:07:48.014 --> 00:07:51.201
but we thought online and
multiplayer-centric games

00:07:51.201 --> 00:07:53.055
would be better as online services

00:07:53.055 --> 00:07:54.903
than as packaged goods.

00:07:54.903 --> 00:07:57.757
Why did we need to sell disks anymore?

00:07:57.757 --> 00:07:59.899
Who buys any of their digital media,

00:07:59.899 --> 00:08:02.610
their other digital
media like music or video

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:05.764
at the store versus downloading?

00:08:05.764 --> 00:08:08.059
And why do games data cost 60 bucks?

00:08:08.059 --> 00:08:09.512
We could make them available for free,

00:08:09.512 --> 00:08:12.501
and then just sell optional
content a la carte.

00:08:12.501 --> 00:08:15.805
And players could opt in
only if they saw value.

00:08:15.805 --> 00:08:18.607
This would shift the burden on to the

00:08:18.607 --> 00:08:21.024
quality of the content and the experience.

00:08:21.024 --> 00:08:24.461
And games on disks were
static, like movies.

00:08:24.461 --> 00:08:27.356
Games would get printed
and then the developers

00:08:27.356 --> 00:08:29.374
would literally move
on to the next project

00:08:29.374 --> 00:08:32.691
or sequel, so we thought games should

00:08:32.691 --> 00:08:34.572
have live development teams.

00:08:34.572 --> 00:08:36.270
Dedicated to evolving the experience

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:39.164
into reacting to user feedback.

00:08:39.164 --> 00:08:40.442
We wanted to build a company that saw

00:08:40.442 --> 00:08:42.142
the world through the eyes of gamers,

00:08:42.142 --> 00:08:43.872
and had a commitment to quality and

00:08:43.872 --> 00:08:46.747
a relentless focus on
the player experience.

00:08:46.747 --> 00:08:48.692
We make mistakes, right?

00:08:48.692 --> 00:08:51.593
As you heard me say in the
talk a little bit earlier,

00:08:51.593 --> 00:08:53.510
making mistakes is kind
of part of our culture,

00:08:53.510 --> 00:08:55.157
because we feel like if we get to a point

00:08:55.157 --> 00:08:56.173
where we're not making mistakes,

00:08:56.173 --> 00:08:58.212
we're spending too much
time covering our butts,

00:08:58.212 --> 00:08:59.993
and we're not moving fast enough,

00:08:59.993 --> 00:09:01.596
and we're not taking enough risks.

00:09:01.596 --> 00:09:04.042
But the key is to not make
the same mistake twice,

00:09:04.042 --> 00:09:06.066
and to learn from our mistakes and to

00:09:06.066 --> 00:09:08.197
constantly strive to get better,

00:09:08.197 --> 00:09:10.867
so that's where we, that's
sort of our philosophy.

00:09:10.867 --> 00:09:12.695
Who in this audience believes,

00:09:12.695 --> 00:09:15.466
without a shred of doubt,

00:09:15.466 --> 00:09:18.549
that nothing, not technology, not IP,

00:09:19.598 --> 00:09:23.191
not patents, not trademarks, not brands,

00:09:23.191 --> 00:09:24.674
not relationships, not any asset

00:09:24.674 --> 00:09:27.757
of a game company is worth more than,

00:09:29.857 --> 00:09:33.874
to our company's success than
the quality of our teams?

00:09:33.874 --> 00:09:35.191
If you believe that without a shadow

00:09:35.191 --> 00:09:36.630
of a doubt raise your hand.

00:09:36.630 --> 00:09:38.431
Okay, a lot of hands, that's awesome.

00:09:38.431 --> 00:09:40.800
Now raise your hand if
that sounds idealistic,

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:42.593
you believe it in your heart,

00:09:42.593 --> 00:09:43.977
and you want to believe it but your mind

00:09:43.977 --> 00:09:46.307
is just kind of full of questions?

00:09:46.307 --> 00:09:49.432
Those other things are pretty important.

00:09:49.432 --> 00:09:52.925
Yeah, we're pretty sure
of this, which is great.

00:09:52.925 --> 00:09:55.425
Now lastly, raise your hand if

00:09:57.069 --> 00:09:59.736
your company, your shareholders,

00:10:00.921 --> 00:10:02.830
your boardrooms all believe this

00:10:02.830 --> 00:10:04.389
and internalize it the same way you do

00:10:04.389 --> 00:10:07.090
without a shred of doubt.

00:10:07.090 --> 00:10:09.496
Okay, a lot less hands.

00:10:09.496 --> 00:10:13.663
Now this is where I think we
have a problem as an industry.

00:10:16.404 --> 00:10:19.446
Because until we all agree,
until all stakeholders

00:10:19.446 --> 00:10:22.374
fully believe in the value

00:10:22.374 --> 00:10:24.874
of our people then we struggle

00:10:26.545 --> 00:10:29.783
to prioritize what really matters.

00:10:29.783 --> 00:10:32.150
And it's going to hold
us back as an industry

00:10:32.150 --> 00:10:34.150
from our full potential.

00:10:35.596 --> 00:10:37.983
- [Marc] It's so rewarding
to see the passion.

00:10:37.983 --> 00:10:41.467
Players do things with our champions

00:10:41.467 --> 00:10:42.883
that we didn't even imagine,

00:10:42.883 --> 00:10:44.170
the designers didn't even imagine.

00:10:44.170 --> 00:10:46.906
And we sort of evolved
that understanding together

00:10:46.906 --> 00:10:48.446
with the community.

00:10:48.446 --> 00:10:49.431
- [Commentator] Miss, oh the flash!

00:10:49.431 --> 00:10:50.708
Got 'em!

00:10:50.708 --> 00:10:53.215
Shally Shall goes down!

00:10:53.215 --> 00:10:56.965
(inspiring electronic music)

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:01.025
- [Brandon] When the community creates

00:11:01.025 --> 00:11:02.773
a piece of content that just

00:11:02.773 --> 00:11:05.481
totally makes you smile or laugh,

00:11:05.481 --> 00:11:06.761
I think what they do for us is they just

00:11:06.761 --> 00:11:10.678
drive this need to live
up to the expectations.

00:11:12.182 --> 00:11:15.171
We got to be worthy of
this kind of passion.

00:11:15.171 --> 00:11:17.655
(audience cheers)

00:11:17.655 --> 00:11:19.921
Sometimes we spend so much time

00:11:19.921 --> 00:11:22.852
thinking about features,
developing something,

00:11:22.852 --> 00:11:25.039
and then we lose perspective,

00:11:25.039 --> 00:11:28.161
and the community can just keep us honest.

00:11:28.161 --> 00:11:30.334
- And those are the times
we try to really acknowledge

00:11:30.334 --> 00:11:32.667
our mistakes and get better.

00:11:33.632 --> 00:11:35.626
I think we both kind of feel a tremendous

00:11:35.626 --> 00:11:37.395
amount of gratitude for the

00:11:37.395 --> 00:11:40.844
incredible ride we've
sort of had together,

00:11:40.844 --> 00:11:44.211
and the moments that we've been touched

00:11:44.211 --> 00:11:46.278
by meeting players and
seeing their passion

00:11:46.278 --> 00:11:49.618
for League and appreciation
for a lot of things.

00:11:49.618 --> 00:11:51.043
What it's done, it did help us

00:11:51.043 --> 00:11:54.250
grow as people and has been incredible.

00:11:54.250 --> 00:11:56.096
- The gratitude that we have is just,

00:11:56.096 --> 00:12:00.926
there's no amount of words
that could express it.

00:12:00.926 --> 00:12:02.059
- Thank you guys so much for watching,

00:12:02.059 --> 00:12:04.579
I made this video because
Dennis Vang asked me to.

00:12:04.579 --> 00:12:07.091
I'm also a big League of Legends fan,

00:12:07.091 --> 00:12:09.880
look me up, Believe Team on
the North American Server,

00:12:09.880 --> 00:12:11.149
if you want to duel.

00:12:11.149 --> 00:12:12.795
If there's someone you'd like to vote on

00:12:12.795 --> 00:12:14.330
for the next Top 10, check out the link

00:12:14.330 --> 00:12:16.594
in the description and
go and have your say.

00:12:16.594 --> 00:12:18.194
Finally, I want to give a quick shout-out

00:12:18.194 --> 00:12:19.883
to Nate Lee Morales,
Nate, thank you so much

00:12:19.883 --> 00:12:21.122
for picking up a copy of my book,

00:12:21.122 --> 00:12:23.424
Your One Word, and for
having me on your podcast,

00:12:23.424 --> 00:12:24.888
it was a ton of fun.

00:12:24.888 --> 00:12:26.581
I really, really appreciate the support,

00:12:26.581 --> 00:12:28.284
and I'm so glad you enjoyed the read.

00:12:28.284 --> 00:12:29.872
So thank you guys again for watching,

00:12:29.872 --> 00:12:31.303
I believe in your, I hope you continue

00:12:31.303 --> 00:12:34.212
to believe in yourself and
whatever your one word is,

00:12:34.212 --> 00:12:36.629
much love, I'll see you soon.

00:12:43.395 --> 00:12:45.838
- You know, as you shift your attention

00:12:45.838 --> 00:12:48.782
to your future, and to what's next,

00:12:48.782 --> 00:12:53.363
I want you to think about
this quote from Henry Ford:

00:12:53.363 --> 00:12:55.542
"Whether you think you can do it,

00:12:55.542 --> 00:12:58.625
"or you think cannot, you are right".

00:13:00.072 --> 00:13:02.917
Those words are more meaningful today

00:13:02.917 --> 00:13:04.638
than I would ever thought

00:13:04.638 --> 00:13:06.899
when I was sitting where you are.

00:13:06.899 --> 00:13:10.545
I had the same worries about
my future as you all have.

00:13:10.545 --> 00:13:12.727
But what I would like to think about

00:13:12.727 --> 00:13:15.038
is that for the vast majority of you,

00:13:15.038 --> 00:13:17.472
the limitations on what you can achieve

00:13:17.472 --> 00:13:20.805
are to a very large degree self-imposed.

00:13:21.827 --> 00:13:24.069
Whether it is an external voice,

00:13:24.069 --> 00:13:26.775
telling you that you
cannot achieve something,

00:13:26.775 --> 00:13:29.538
or it's that internal
voice of fear and of doubt

00:13:29.538 --> 00:13:32.696
that prevents you from
pursuing your passion.

00:13:32.696 --> 00:13:35.467
These can often be more limiting factors

00:13:35.467 --> 00:13:38.826
than the actual career world itself.

00:13:38.826 --> 00:13:40.380
We think it's incredibly important

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:43.215
to be connected to the players.

00:13:43.215 --> 00:13:46.557
It has to guide everything we do,

00:13:46.557 --> 00:13:48.132
every decision that we make,

00:13:48.132 --> 00:13:49.978
it has to start with
the player philosophy,

00:13:49.978 --> 00:13:52.366
and then we sort of work
backwards from there,

00:13:52.366 --> 00:13:53.797
and we don't always get it right,

00:13:53.797 --> 00:13:55.669
but the closer we are with the community,

00:13:55.669 --> 00:13:56.740
the more we'll understand it,

00:13:56.740 --> 00:13:58.512
like some of my favorite moments

00:13:58.512 --> 00:14:01.327
are meeting players up close and personal

00:14:01.327 --> 00:14:03.517
at the trade shows that we're at,

00:14:03.517 --> 00:14:04.987
the e-sports events that we're at.

00:14:04.987 --> 00:14:06.622
I've had so many phenomenal experiences

00:14:06.622 --> 00:14:09.360
getting to know, getting
to know our players

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:12.066
and I think that's invaluable.

00:14:12.066 --> 00:14:14.626
It's a phenomenal resource
for us as developers

00:14:14.626 --> 00:14:17.126
to get that kind of feedback.

